(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-3-pulmonary-quiz-version-5"]={"title":"Pulmonary Physiology — Quiz 4 (30 Questions)","category":"Physiology","questions":[{"q":"During exercise, pulmonary artery pressure rises only modestly despite a substantial increase in pulmonary blood flow, mainly because...","choices":["recruitment and distension of pulmonary vessels sharply lower pulmonary vascular resistance as flow increases","the pulmonary circulation is entirely unaffected by any rise in cardiac output during exercise","pulmonary vascular resistance rises proportionally with flow, keeping pressure changes large","alveolar hypoxia occurring during exercise triggers widespread pulmonary vasodilation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. As pulmonary blood flow rises during exercise, recruitment of previously closed capillaries and distension of already-open vessels sharply reduce pulmonary vascular resistance, which is why pulmonary artery pressure rises only modestly even as flow increases substantially. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 46"},{"q":"Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction — the lung's local vasoconstrictive response to alveolar hypoxia — serves the overall physiological purpose of...","choices":["maximizing blood flow specifically toward the poorly ventilated hypoxic regions of lung","diverting blood flow away from poorly ventilated alveoli toward better-ventilated regions, improving overall gas exchange","permanently damaging the hypoxic alveoli so that gas exchange never resumes there","having essentially no measurable effect on overall ventilation-perfusion matching"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. By constricting vessels supplying poorly ventilated (hypoxic) alveoli, the lung redirects blood flow toward better-ventilated regions, improving the overall matching of ventilation to perfusion (V/Q matching) and thus overall gas exchange efficiency. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 43"},{"q":"Compared with systemic capillaries, pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure (about 7 mmHg) is considerably lower than plasma osmotic pressure (about 28 mmHg). This relationship is functionally important because it helps ensure that...","choices":["fluid continuously and heavily floods the alveoli under normal conditions","the alveoli normally remain relatively dry, favoring efficient gas exchange","gas exchange is impaired due to constant interstitial fluid accumulation","the pulmonary capillaries essentially never filter any fluid outward"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Because plasma osmotic pressure so strongly outweighs pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure, net Starling forces favor only a small, easily lymphatically-drained amount of filtration — keeping the alveolar-capillary interface thin and dry, which is essential for efficient gas diffusion. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 48"},{"q":"The three-zone model of pulmonary blood flow describes how gravity, alveolar pressure, and vascular pressures interact to create regional differences in perfusion. This model helps explain why, in the upright lung, the...","choices":["apex (top) can have relatively reduced perfusion compared with regions lower in the lung","apex and base always receive completely identical blood flow at every posture","ventilation is affected by gravity while perfusion remains entirely unaffected","base of the lung receives essentially no blood flow under any condition"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The three-zone model illustrates how the relationship between alveolar pressure and pulmonary arterial/venous pressures varies by lung height, contributing to the observed pattern in which upper lung regions (apex) receive relatively less perfusion than lower regions, given gravity's effect on the low-pressure pulmonary vasculature. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 45"},{"q":"Boyle's law, which underlies the pressure changes driving airflow into and out of the lungs, states that at a constant temperature, the pressure of a gas is...","choices":["directly proportional to its volume","unrelated to its volume","inversely proportional to its volume","proportional to the square of its volume"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Boyle's law states that, at constant temperature, gas pressure is inversely proportional to volume — so when the thoracic cavity (and thus lung volume) expands during inspiration, intra-alveolar pressure falls below atmospheric pressure, drawing air in; the reverse occurs during expiration. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 8"},{"q":"Dalton's law, relevant to how gases behave in the mixture of air, states that the total pressure of a gas mixture equals...","choices":["the partial pressure of only the most abundant gas in the mixture","the sum of the partial pressures of each individual gas in the mixture","a value entirely independent of the individual gases present","the average, not the sum, of each gas's partial pressure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Dalton's law states that the total pressure exerted by a mixture of gases equals the sum of the partial pressures each individual gas would exert if it alone occupied the same volume — the basis for calculating partial pressures like PO2 and PCO2 within atmospheric or alveolar air. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 9"},{"q":"Bronchiolar diameter, which helps regulate airway resistance, is controlled in part by the autonomic nervous system such that sympathetic stimulation typically causes...","choices":["bronchodilation","contraction of the diaphragm specifically","no change in bronchiolar diameter","bronchoconstriction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sympathetic stimulation (via beta-2 adrenergic receptors on bronchiolar smooth muscle) typically causes bronchodilation, widening the airways and reducing resistance — the opposite of parasympathetic stimulation, which causes bronchoconstriction. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 11"},{"q":"Parasympathetic stimulation of the airways, in contrast to sympathetic stimulation, typically causes...","choices":["contraction of the external intercostal muscles","relaxation of the diaphragm","bronchoconstriction","bronchodilation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Parasympathetic stimulation (via muscarinic receptors on bronchiolar smooth muscle) causes bronchoconstriction, narrowing the airways and increasing resistance — the opposite effect of sympathetic stimulation, which causes bronchodilation. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 11"},{"q":"Timed volumes, such as forced expiratory volume in 1 second (FEV1), are clinically useful measurements because, unlike a simple static lung volume, they capture...","choices":["nothing meaningfully different from an untimed static lung volume measurement","how quickly air can be moved out of the lungs, reflecting airway resistance and lung mechanics over time","only the composition of alveolar gas, with no volume or flow information","only the total amount of air present in the lungs at one single instant"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Because timed volumes measure how much air is exhaled within a specific time frame (such as 1 second), they capture the dynamic rate of airflow — which is sensitive to airway resistance and the mechanical properties of the lung/chest wall — information a simple static volume measurement alone cannot provide. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 29"},{"q":"Physiological dead space differs from anatomic dead space in that physiological dead space also includes...","choices":["only the trachea and main bronchi, exactly matching anatomic dead space","only alveoli that are simultaneously well-ventilated and well-perfused","the entire alveolar volume of the lung, regardless of any blood flow","ventilated alveoli that receive little or no blood flow, in addition to the conducting airways"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. While anatomic dead space refers only to the conducting airways (which never participate in gas exchange), physiological dead space is a broader concept that also includes any ventilated alveoli that are poorly or not perfused — air reaching them is 'wasted' just as if it never reached an alveolus at all. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 34"},{"q":"The respiratory center's control of breathing is organized as a three-part system: the medullary rhythmicity area, the pneumotaxic center, and the...","choices":["hypothalamus","cerebellar cortex","carotid body","apneustic center"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The classic three-part organization of the brainstem respiratory centers includes the medullary rhythmicity area (setting the basic breathing rhythm), the pneumotaxic center in the pons (limiting inspiration duration/rate), and the apneustic center (promoting deeper, more prolonged inspiration when unopposed by the pneumotaxic center). — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 88"},{"q":"The pneumotaxic center's main influence on the basic breathing rhythm is to...","choices":["increase tidal volume with no effect on timing","control only expiratory muscle contraction, not inspiration","completely stop all breathing activity indefinitely once activated","limit the duration of inspiration, helping set breathing rate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The pneumotaxic center in the pons sends signals that limit inspiration's duration, effectively controlling how quickly one breath transitions to the next and thereby helping set overall respiratory rate. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 88"},{"q":"Carotid body glomus cells sense arterial oxygen levels through a specialized O2-sensing mechanism, allowing the peripheral chemoreceptors to detect...","choices":["a fall in arterial PO2, triggering increased ventilation","core body temperature exclusively","a rise in arterial PO2, triggering decreased ventilation only","blood glucose levels exclusively, unrelated to oxygen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Carotid body glomus cells are specialized to sense falling arterial PO2 (particularly once it drops substantially below normal), triggering an increase in ventilation via the peripheral chemoreceptor pathway — a backup oxygen-sensing drive to breathe, distinct from the dominant CO2/pH-based central drive. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 95"},{"q":"Under conditions of moderate exercise, ventilation increases in close proportion to metabolic rate/CO2 production, such that arterial PCO2 typically...","choices":["becomes completely unregulated once exercise begins, rising and falling unpredictably","stays remarkably close to its resting value, despite the large rise in ventilation and CO2 production","falls to dangerously low levels during essentially any exercise intensity","rises dramatically and continuously throughout the entire period of moderate exercise"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. During moderate exercise, the rise in ventilation is so well matched to the increase in metabolic CO2 production that arterial PCO2 is kept remarkably close to its normal resting value, rather than rising or falling significantly — a testament to the precision of ventilatory control. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 100"},{"q":"Immediately at the very onset of exercise (before metabolic byproducts like CO2 have had time to rise significantly), ventilation often increases abruptly, suggesting that this initial rise is driven partly by...","choices":["chemoreceptor detection of a large arterial CO2 rise that occurs essentially instantaneously","complete cessation of all central chemoreceptor activity at the onset of exercise","a simultaneous, instantaneous drop in arterial PO2 the moment exercise starts","neural/anticipatory input (e.g., from motor cortex and joint/muscle receptors) rather than chemoreceptor feedback alone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Because ventilation can rise abruptly at the very start of exercise — before arterial blood gases have meaningfully changed — this initial increase is thought to be driven substantially by neural (feedforward) signals from the motor cortex and receptors in joints/muscles, working alongside (and initially ahead of) the chemoreceptor feedback that sustains ventilation as exercise continues. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 100"},{"q":"Expiratory reserve volume (ERV) is defined as the...","choices":["additional air that can be forcibly exhaled after a normal, quiet expiration","air that remains in the lungs even after a maximal forced expiration","maximum air that can be inspired after a normal tidal inspiration","air that is simply moved in and out during normal, quiet resting breathing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Expiratory reserve volume is the extra volume of air that can still be forcibly exhaled after the end of a normal, quiet (tidal) expiration, representing part of the lung's reserve capacity beyond typical resting breathing. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 26"},{"q":"Because the pulmonary circulation operates at much lower pressure than the systemic circulation, the right ventricle (which generates pulmonary artery pressure) is correspondingly...","choices":["irrelevant to pulmonary artery pressure, which is generated elsewhere","identical in wall thickness and pressure generation to the left ventricle","thinner-walled and generates less pressure than the left ventricle","thicker-walled and generates more pressure than the left ventricle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Since the pulmonary circulation's low resistance requires only a modest driving pressure (mean around 15 mmHg) to achieve the same flow as the systemic circulation, the right ventricle is thinner-walled and generates substantially less pressure than the thick-walled left ventricle, which must drive blood through the much higher-resistance systemic circuit. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 39"},{"q":"The apneustic center, one of the classic three components of brainstem respiratory control alongside the medullary rhythmicity area and pneumotaxic center, tends to promote...","choices":["complete cessation of breathing (apnea) as its normal, unopposed function","deeper, more prolonged inspiration when not opposed by the pneumotaxic center","shorter, shallower inspiration under all conditions, regardless of opposition","expiratory muscle contraction exclusively, with no effect on inspiration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The apneustic center promotes strong, sustained inspiratory effort (deep, prolonged inspiration); under normal conditions, the pneumotaxic center limits/opposes this tendency, helping produce the normal rhythmic breathing pattern rather than prolonged inspiratory holds. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 88"},{"q":"Total lung capacity (TLC) is defined as the sum of...","choices":["only functional residual capacity","vital capacity and residual volume","only tidal volume and inspiratory reserve volume","only residual volume"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Total lung capacity equals vital capacity (the maximum air that can be voluntarily moved in and out) plus residual volume (the air that always remains in the lungs even after maximal forced expiration, and thus cannot be measured by simple spirometry). — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 28"},{"q":"According to the gas laws governing airflow, air moves from a region of...","choices":["a region of any pressure to another region of identical pressure","lower pressure to higher pressure","higher pressure to lower pressure","low resistance to high resistance regardless of pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Like any fluid, air flows down its pressure gradient, from a region of higher pressure to one of lower pressure — which is why alveolar pressure must fall below atmospheric pressure during inspiration for air to flow into the lungs, and rise above atmospheric during expiration for air to flow out. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 12"},{"q":"During exercise, even though pulmonary blood flow can increase several-fold over resting levels, gas exchange typically remains efficient because...","choices":["recruitment/distension increase capillary transit time capacity and surface area, keeping diffusion adequate despite faster overall flow","blood simply moves too fast through the capillaries for any gas exchange to occur at all","the respiratory membrane becomes dramatically thicker during exercise, slowing diffusion intentionally","ventilation decreases proportionally in order to compensate for the increased blood flow"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Recruitment of additional capillaries and distension of existing ones during exercise expand the total pulmonary capillary blood volume and surface area, which helps preserve adequate time and surface area for gas diffusion even as overall blood flow (and thus average flow velocity) increases substantially. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 63"},{"q":"Inspiratory reserve volume (IRV) is defined as the...","choices":["additional air that can be forcibly exhaled after a normal tidal expiration","additional air that can be forcibly inhaled after a normal, quiet inspiration","air that remains in the lungs after a maximal forced expiration","air that is simply moved in and out during normal, quiet resting breathing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Inspiratory reserve volume is the extra volume of air that can still be forcibly inhaled beyond the end of a normal, quiet (tidal) inspiration, representing part of the lung's reserve capacity for deeper breathing when needed. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 26"},{"q":"Central chemoreceptors in the brainstem are separated from arterial blood by the blood-brain barrier, which is relatively impermeable to hydrogen ions but freely permeable to...","choices":["carbon dioxide","hydrogen ions directly, with no need for CO2 involvement","glucose exclusively, unrelated to acid-base status","oxygen exclusively, with no role for CO2"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The blood-brain barrier is freely permeable to CO2 (but poorly permeable to H+ directly), so CO2 diffuses into the cerebrospinal fluid, where it combines with water to form carbonic acid and releases H+ locally — this is how central chemoreceptors indirectly sense arterial CO2 changes via a local drop in CSF pH, even though H+ itself cannot easily cross the barrier. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 92"},{"q":"Fluid accumulating within the interstitial space of the respiratory membrane (as opposed to remaining confined to well-drained pulmonary capillaries) would primarily impair gas exchange by...","choices":["improving diffusion by adding a favorable fluid medium for gas transport","increasing the diffusion distance gases must travel across the respiratory membrane","having no meaningful effect on gas exchange efficiency","decreasing the total surface area available for gas exchange only, with no effect on diffusion distance"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Because the respiratory membrane is normally extremely thin (about 0.2 micrometers) to favor rapid diffusion, any interstitial fluid accumulation adds extra distance gases must diffuse across, slowing gas exchange even though the total membrane surface area may be unchanged. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 59"},{"q":"Peripheral chemoreceptors, in addition to sensing arterial PO2, also respond to blood pH, such that a fall in pH (acidosis) tends to...","choices":["stimulate a decrease in ventilation via the peripheral chemoreceptors","stimulate an increase in ventilation via the peripheral chemoreceptors","have essentially no measurable effect on peripheral chemoreceptor activity","only affect central, never peripheral, chemoreceptor activity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A fall in arterial pH (acidosis) stimulates peripheral chemoreceptors (in addition to central mechanisms), increasing ventilation — part of the respiratory system's broader role in helping compensate for acid-base disturbances by adjusting CO2 elimination. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 98"},{"q":"Emphysema and pulmonary fibrosis affect lung compliance in opposite directions: emphysema (which destroys elastic tissue) tends to make the lungs...","choices":["more compliant than normal (easier to inflate, but with reduced elastic recoil)","identical in compliance to a lung affected by pulmonary fibrosis","completely unaffected in their compliance despite the tissue destruction","less compliant than normal, stiffer and harder to inflate than usual"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Because emphysema destroys the lung's elastic connective tissue, the lungs become more compliant (easier to stretch/inflate) than normal — but this comes at the cost of reduced elastic recoil, which impairs the passive expiratory process that normally relies on that recoil. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 21"},{"q":"Because the pulmonary circulation must accept the entire cardiac output (all blood returning to the heart passes through the lungs before reaching the systemic circulation), the pulmonary vasculature's low resistance is functionally essential mainly to...","choices":["allow the right ventricle to routinely work harder than the left ventricle","reduce the total volume of blood that the lungs actually need to accept","prevent the right ventricle from having to generate excessively high pressures to drive that full flow","eliminate any physiological need for the right ventricle to exist at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Because 100% of cardiac output must pass through the pulmonary circulation (unlike systemic organs, which receive only a fraction of cardiac output each), the pulmonary vasculature's low resistance is essential so the right ventricle does not need to generate the very high pressures that would otherwise be required to drive the full cardiac output through a high-resistance circuit. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 39"},{"q":"The extremely large combined surface area of the roughly 300 million alveoli (comparable to a tennis court) is functionally important because it...","choices":["has no meaningful effect on the overall rate of pulmonary gas exchange","reduces the total volume of air that the lungs are able to hold","is important mainly for sound production during vocalization and speech","maximizes the total area available for simultaneous gas diffusion between air and blood"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Spreading gas exchange across an enormous combined alveolar surface area allows a large volume of O2 and CO2 to diffuse simultaneously across many alveoli at once, dramatically increasing the lung's overall capacity for gas exchange compared with a single large air sac of the same total volume. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 57"},{"q":"A decrease in lung volume (such as toward residual volume) tends to increase airway resistance mainly because...","choices":["smaller lung volumes always widen the airways, meaningfully decreasing resistance","surfactant production stops entirely once lung volume falls below normal","airway resistance is completely independent of any change in lung volume","smaller lung volumes exert less outward radial traction on airway walls, allowing them to narrow"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. At smaller lung volumes, the surrounding lung tissue exerts less outward radial traction (tethering) on the airway walls, allowing the airways to narrow — and because resistance rises steeply as radius falls (resistance being inversely proportional to radius to the fourth power), even modest narrowing at low lung volumes produces a substantial rise in airway resistance. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 14"},{"q":"Because pulmonary capillary pressure (about 7 mmHg) is so much lower than the roughly 25 mmHg plasma osmotic pressure gradient available to oppose it, small changes in either pulmonary capillary pressure or plasma osmotic pressure can meaningfully shift the...","choices":["net Starling filtration balance, affecting how much fluid filters into the pulmonary interstitium","diffusing capacity for carbon monoxide exclusively, unrelated to fluid balance","basic rhythm of breathing generated within the medullary rhythmicity area","total pulmonary blood flow, independent of any fluid-exchange consequences"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Because normal net filtration pressure across pulmonary capillaries is only about +1 mmHg — a small residual left over from much larger opposing outward and inward forces — relatively modest changes in capillary pressure or plasma osmotic pressure can proportionally shift this delicate balance considerably, affecting how much fluid filters into the interstitium and, ultimately, whether lymphatic drainage can keep pace. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 48"}]};
